I've read Charles Murray's "Losing Ground," which presents a compelling
case that the decline in "values" seen from about the mid-50s on is in fact
related to the increased role of government.

We've written literally megabytes of articles on this general theme, so let
me tell just one anecdote (I forget whether Murray also brings it up).

At one point, taking "relief" or "general assistance" was shameful and was
greatly despised. It was only for the poorest of the poor. Most families
sought aid from their families or churches, and so were motivated to better
their situations as quickly as possible.

By the 1960s, a legion of social workers began spreading the meme that the
welfare money was theirs for the taking, that they were "entitled" to it.
High taxes on the "rich" were only fair, since the rich had exploited the
poor. The welfare system was just a way for the poor to obtain justice. And
the money came from an impersonal government office, with no shame
attached.

At about the same time as blacks were pouring into the cities (the great
migrations in the 1950s and 60s), the best jobs were moving to the suburbs,
by and large. This left many, many black males unemployed in the inner
cities. What they were unable to provide, the State provided. The State
took on the role of provider.

The triple whammy was the rule that welfare money (and AFDC, WICC, etc.)
could not go to complete families (with husbands present). The 14-year-old
illiterate black girl could immediately get her own place and $700 a month,
tax-free, merely by getting pregant. A second child raised the payment to
$1000, and so on. (These are in current dollars, and are my best
recollection of welfare levels.) This broke the family system and created
multigenerational welfare.

A criminal system. With no solution save for destroying it by crypto
anarchic subversion of the tax system. 

-Tim May
