Date: Thu, 11 Mar 1993 03:11:32 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: OLDIES: Optimism

[this is an edited version of three old posts--NS]

Libertarians & anarchists can sometimes be found expressing the
fear (or hope) that "the system" is about to collapse or go bankrupt.
Ecofascists often warn us of the coming ecological crash.  What
silliness; what gross underestimation of civilization.

Economy:
The Third World has both the technological and educational basis for a 
huge surge in economic growth in the forseeable future.  I
predict 8%/year growth in Asia and 5%/year in the rest
of the developing world for at least the next ten years, and
greater rates of growth after that.

In turn, the developed world will be able to live off very 
cheap imports and concentrate on computers, biotech and info-service 
industries.  The only limits to first-world growth seem to be 
(a) our ridiculously low birth rates, which mean far fewer highly 
educated people coming up through the ranks than could be, and (b) 
the speed at which we reeducate the work force to the info services 
economy.

Despite Clinton's severe interference, I see the % of GNP we spend on 
medical care continuing to climb.  It's the only major sector we 
consider a _need_, as opposed to a luxury, that is not being satiated 
in our very wealthy society.  In the short term Clinton can really
screw it up, though.

Technology:
The much-touted "failure" of AI may be 
about to blow up in some critic's faces as emergent
computation invades the scientific and engineering fields,
performing design work formerly reserved for geniuses or
just plain impossible, and discovering new scientific laws.
Already, a few genetic algorithms have re-discovered
several scientific laws and designed algorithms that outperform
anything any human has devised, even geniuses like Knuth.
Meanwhile, engineers & scientists now have CD-ROM abstracts,
e-mail, Mathematica, etc. at their disposal.  Has anybody
besides me noticed the competition getting _smarter_ in
the last few years?

Manufacturing capital will more and more become self-bootstrapping,
a massive distributed version of "self-replicating factories".
Japanese Manzac (self-milling) and Fanuc (self-assembling) plants
point in this direction. E-mag floating robots, 3D printing, real-time
intelligent control with fuzzy logic, neural nets, & subsumption
architectures applied to machine tools, and many more mundane simple 
improvements in automation will combine to produce a self-bootstrapping
manufacturing base that will reduce the cost of manufacturing in
half every decade of the 21st centry.  The economy will become dominated
by services, which themselves will become increasingly prone to
productivity improvements as we learn to use software correctly.

More of the both:
The market's arent't dumb.  Bond prices aren't soaring, and
interest rates declining, because U.S. bankruptcy is in the offing.
The U.S. federal debt is less than one year's GNP.  Many families 
have mortgages three times their annual income, the income can stop at 
any time due to layoffs, and they aren't allowed to inflate their way out 
of it.  The U.S. government is about about an order of magnitude
away from any serious debt crisis.

The Third World growth will play a major role, since many wealthy
in the U.S., Japan, and Europe are invested there.  Their profits 
provide a ready market for more U.S. debt and a source of taxes.
Cheap goods made in the Third World provide a safety net that
allows the U.S. government to cut Social Security and welfare, raise 
taxes, etc. without throwing the voters into poverty.  The Third
World also provides ready TAZ's to manufacture smart drugs, do
genetic engineering, keep profits, etc. if developed countries go nutsos 
and ban these productive activities.

Baby boom retirement won't be as big a problem as it has been
made out to be.  The leading edge of the Baby Boom doesn't hit 65 until 
2014, and the trailing edge until 2029.  By that time smart drugs,
Alzheimer's & Parkinson's cures, etc. will likely have pushed
a worker's productive years to 80 or beyond.  So indeed, 
this is a _long term_ problem. 

There will always be bubbles.  Recently the bubble of what used to be
the most heavily capitalized stock market in the world in Tokyo
burst, crashing to half its former value.  Did that ruin the Japanese 
government or economy, or the value of the yen?  It did not.  The
yen is strong as ever, the Japanese economy is growing at only 
a slightly slower pace than before (following a long term trend,
due to their declining birthrate), and the government continues
to function normally despite a debt load similar to the U.S.'s.
No anarchocapitalist takeover in Japan.

We're going to have to bring down statism by hard work 
and continuous, gradual improvement.  The current statist/capitalist 
symbiosis is far too strong to "crash under its own weight", or "reveal 
its inherent contradictions" as the Marxists hoped.  

